TY  - JOUR
TI  - Restoration of potentially contaminated sites or development of undeveloped areas Comparison of alternatives for commercial use with the help of a qualitative, quantitative and monetary assessment of social potentials and effects
AU  - Burmeier, H. (comp.)
AU  - Doetsch, P. (Focon-Ingenieurgesellschaft fuer Umwelttechnologie- und Forschungskonsulting mbH, Aachen (Germany))
AU  - Ruepke, A. (WCI Umwelttechnik GmbH, Wennigsen (Germany))
AU  - Umweltbundesamt, Berlin (Germany)
PY  - 1998
JO  - OpenGrey (Institut de l'Information Scientifique et Technique)
AB  - The research program, presented in this report, included the examination of consequences for the development of an industrial or commercial site either on greenfields (natural, undeveloped area) or on brownfields (former used sites by industrial activities with potential for contamination). For this purpose, the above mentioned alternatives of land use have been compared and evaluated in all aspects. In the past the evaluation criteria regarding future useability factors was limited to operation specific economical criteria. The new assessment method developed by the research program considers also the qualitative balancing of ecological, urban development and land planning issues. For the first time the revitalisation of formerly used and decontaminated land was analyzed and compared with the consumption of natural land not only from the perspective of business management but also with regard to all social elements and natural resources. For the evaluation a scaling method has been developed to describe the initial status of both alternatives and the effects according to the future commercial or industrial use. A ranking of describing and evaluation factors has been used to give quantitative scaling to the total worth, gains or losses. Following this quantification all decision relevant criteria have been monetarized by adding a specific cost number. This approach allows to reduce all qualitative aspects of using different kind of land to one single factor, the total costing and land development in regard to the public economy. The applicability of the methodical approach has been proved by four actual examples of site development in the cities of Luebeck and Dresden. The evaluation of land development costs at these four sites was resulting in the first financial approach to compare the use of brownfields with the use of green, natural land. Including the costs not only for the business operation itself but also for ecological and other relevant factors shows, that revitalisation of brownfields can be a big advantage overall. (orig.)
