TY  - CHAP
TI  - Varieties of Networks and Policy Implementation
AU  - Edward Peck
AU  - Perri Six
PY  - 2006
JO  - Palgrave Macmillan UK eBooks
AB  - Implementing almost any policy requires the co-ordination of a great many organisations (Hanf and Scharpf, 1978; Benson, 1975, 1982; O’Toole, 1988, 1993). Even policies that at first seem to involve very few organisations — for example, creating a single body to close down a public authority (Dunsire, 1978) or regulating the prices of a monopoly utility (Bishop, Kay and Mayer, 1995) — require complicated manoeuvring between many campaign groups and rival organisations. Indeed, the now-canonical studies of implementation in the 1960s and 1970s identified as a critical source of implementation failure the difficulties involved in aligning the interests, institutional constraints, values, perceptions of risk and opportunities, capabilities and priorities of many disparate organisations (Pressman and Wildavsky, 1984 [1973]; Van Meter and van Horn, 1975). While “top-down” theories of implementation continued to stress the problems inherent in designing and operating inter-organisational relations (Mazmanian and Sabatier, 1981, 1983; Hogwood and Gunn, 1984; Goggin et al., 1990), the “bottom-up” tradition urged resignation to the inevitability of disappointment and the need for the scaling down of the high expectations that marked the “top-down” school (Elmore, 1978; Lipsky, 1980; Hjern and Hull, 1982; Barrett and Fudge, 1981; Porter and Hjern, 1981; Stoker, 1991). Empirical studies of major initiatives in horizontal inter-agency working from the 1970s and 1980s broadly shared the common “pessimistic” view that inter-organisational working was unavoidably problematic, principally because the tight control necessary in “top-down” approaches could not be achieved (Challis et al., 1988) and there were thought to be few alternative approaches that could support faithful execution of central policy-makers’ intentions. These keywords were added by machine and not by the authors. This process is experimental and the keywords may be updated as the learning algorithm improves.
