TY  - JOUR
TI  - Business Processes Re-design Using Accountability Paradigm with IDEF Modeling and Simulation
AU  - Chuan‐Jun Su
PY  - 2008
JO  - 品質學報
AB  - Business processes dictate the way firms conducting business and encapsulate the efficiency and performance of business operations. Companies constantly strive for designing better business processes to streamline business throughput, to reduce costs or work lead-time, or to maximize output. Process designers often focus on the details of tasks/activities involved and overlook the true objectives that the processes are designed for. In this paper, a new approach of Accountability Driven Paradigm (ADP) for business process redesign is described. The ADP enables the designers and users to focus on the requirements and rationales of the process design, before settling into selecting and committing to a set of activities. Consequently, the ultimate objectives of the business process can be realized and achieved. To effectively implement ADP, IDEF modeling and simulation techniques are used to support process reengineering as system engineering. ”As-is” and ”to-be” models are constructed using IDEF based on the concept of ADP. These models are subsequently compared via logical evaluation, static analysis and dynamic simulation to achieve the expected performance improvement.
