JO  - SSRN Electronic Journal
AB  - Across sectors of the global economy, de-territorial governance has emerged as a new instrument for addressing pressing social and environmental problems. Although well-suited to overcoming the challenge of reaching agreements among states to address problems transcending national borders, these initiatives create new boundaries based on what problems they choose to focus on and which actors they choose to regulate. Specialization is not inherently problematic. De-territorial governance can focus attention on the problems of a single issue area and build capacity among actors to resolve its problems, but equally a particular focus can create more problems than it fixes. Using programs from the forest, fisheries, and coffee sectors, this paper explores the implications of these issue-area boundaries and offers recommendations for future coordination strategies that draw from work in international relations and public administration.
