TY  - JOUR
TI  - Redevelopment of Urban Brownfields - Examples from Germany and Poland
AU  - Adam Radzimski
AU  - Josh Richert
AU  - A Zabojnik
PY  - 2010
AB  - It might be surprising that in the times when cities sprawl the number of unused land is increasing. The increasing number of brownfields (derelict land) is a characteristic feature of post-industrial era, but brownfields are not only former industrial areas. There are also former military lands, unused railway areas and many other types of brownfields. One feature they all have in common is that they need some kind of public intervention to bring them back to beneficiary use (compare CABERNET-brownfield definition). Brownfields located within city boundaries can be referred to as “urban brownfields”. They are a very complex phenomenon because there are many actors involved, first of all the owner (local or absentee), city authorities, developers, NGOs, inhabitants and other. They are also very important for the development of city as a whole, especially in the perspective of sustainability. The interest in redevelopment of urban brownfields is growing because of the general trend towards sustainable development in the politics, economy and society. Sustainability in a land use context means the reduction of land consumption and land recycling. From the ecological perspective it is better to reuse already urbanised land than to encourage urban sprawl. This is a priority of brownfields over greenfields, or in other words a preference for inward development over outward development. Redevelopment of urban brownfields, although there are costs and risks associated with this process, brings many positive changes. It improves the quality of life, increases land values and gives a better overall image of the city. In our times when the resources become scarce, reusing (recycling) of urban land helps to save the natural environment from urbanisation. However, this strategy requires a new approach to the urban planning. Old instruments need to be verified and new instruments need to be found in order to support the inward urban development. This paper aims to compare the overall conditions of brownfield redevelopment in Germany and Poland as well as local strategies in selected cities. This dual approach is necessary because the strategies of brownfield redevelopment depend on the political and legal framework on the one hand and on the specific local conditions on the other. Both the general and local conditions may encourage or discourage the redevelopment of brownfields. For example, a planning policy which limits urban sprawl would be an incentive for the redevelopment of brownfields and vice versa. The local conditions are very diverse and in a combination with different actors’ constellations they lead to different redevelopment strategies. For example, former industrial city with declining population and large unemployment does not have large demand for new housing or office space, but rather for green areas. Growing cities on the contrary have got large demand on construction land on the one hand and on green land on the other, so that land use conflicts may occur.
UR  - http://iaps.architexturez.net//doc/oai-iaps-id-iaps-21-2010-238
