AB  - Inter-municipal cooperative arrangements vary in shape, scope and level of integration. They result from the political initiative of diverse, often opposite, agents and present different forms in nature and in the theoretical lenses used to study them. This chapter explores its diversity, demonstrates why comparative studies have been discouraged and offers a summary of the main differences and characteristics of those entities. Though diversity is evident in European countries, a different set of problems shows up when comparative analysis of inter-municipal cooperation is attempted: diversity in theory. Furthermore, crossing municipal borders may lead to confusion among the citizenry, since territorial and polity borders often do not match the territories of policy decision and implementation. These three main difficulties in research are discussed in the chapter. These keywords were added by machine and not by the authors. This process is experimental and the keywords may be updated as the learning algorithm improves.
