TY  - CHAP
TI  - “The Competitive Advantage of the Inner City”
AU  - Michael E. Porter
PY  - 2020
AB  - In this 1995 article, from the Harvard Business Review , Harvard Business School professor and the director of Harvard’s Institute for Strategy and Competitiveness Michael Porter describes what he considers to be the strengths of inner-city neighborhoods and how government can help private business build on these strengths to revitalize cities. Porter is a neo-liberal who believes the private market can solve most social needs. He attacks approaches to inner-city decline, unemployment, and resulting social problems based on a social model aimed at the individual as ineffective. He argues that government job creation and training programs in inner-city neighborhoods have been fragmented and inefficient. Subsidies, preference programs, and expensive efforts to stimulate economic activity in inner-city neighborhoods have not worked. Porter blames the high costs and difficulties of doing business in inner cities largely on government regulation and anti-business attitudes. Accordingly, he argues that local governments can and should improve the economic climate of inner-city neighborhoods and make them more attractive for private investment by reducing regulation. Porter argues that the best way to address inner-city problems lies in encouraging private entrepreneurs to build successful private for-profit businesses that will provide jobs. He sees four true advantages to inner-city locations: (1) their strategic location, (2) local market demand the areas themselves possess, (3) possibilities of integration with regional job clusters, and (4) an industrious labor force that is eager to work. Firms that can best exploit these inner-city advantages, Porter believes, can turn a profit without government assistance.
DO  - 10.4324/9780429261732-38
UR  - https://doi.org/10.4324/9780429261732-38
