TI  - Implementation
AU  - Larry N. Gerston
PY  - 2021
AB  - The importance of policymaking notwithstanding, it’s worthless without being put into action. Implementation is the task of converting policy commitments into practice. The process is anything but automatic. In fact, implementation is fraught with challenges, obstacles, and uncertainties. Typically, most implementation is carried out in the bureaucracy, a menagerie of agencies defined by their initials. For a bureaucracy to succeed, it must be able to understand the policy, have resources, have worked with a limited number of actors, and demonstrate accountability. Importantly, implementing bureaucrats must guard against postdecision bargaining, new priorities, and poor oversight. Sometimes, bureaucrats may become the policymakers if traditional policymakers hand off their authority.
