AB  - Interim uses allow for flexibility and experimentation in urban development. This is especially valuable in shrinking cities, where properties often remain vacant for a long time. Interim uses, in the form of pop up projects for vacant buildings and sites, can deliver social and economic benefits in the short-term and lead to community-responsive development in the long-term. This chapter offers a brief history of interim uses in shrinking cities and provides case studies of pop up projects designed to foster community engagement, envision development alternatives, and enliven neighbourhoods struggling with depopulation and disinvestment. The chapter also weighs the costs and challenges of interim uses, with guidelines for assessing pop up projects and measuring outcomes.
