AB  - Abstract This chapter turns introduces the idea of urban propertyscapes, which refers to patterns of residential and commercial property development in a given city, how this is arranged across urban space, and how it relates to land regimes and the politics of regulation. After examining the post-conflict evolution of the real estate sector in the three cities, the chapter delineates basic incentives and constraints and how these have channelled ever more resources into the ‘secondary circuit’ of capital, drawing resources away from more productive investment. It then considers questions of urban housing provision, particularly in Addis Ababa, the site of one of Africa’s most extraordinary state-driven housing experiments, and considers why so little urban housing has been built in the other cases. In a concluding comparative discussion, it argues that different distributions of power and strategies of legitimation are central to accounting for the different emergent urban propertyscapes across the region.
