TY  - CHAP
TI  - The Invisible beyond Visible
AU  - Gihan Karunaratne
AU  - Jagath Munasinghe
AU  - Tanzil Shafique
PY  - 2023
AB  - Urban regeneration is often celebrated by planners and professionals – particularly those with a neoliberal agenda – as a policy that has the potential to attract valuable investment, generate wealth, and increase the overall quality of urban life. This chapter presents the opposing view, that regeneration projects target disadvantaged communities and deprive them of their right to the city. It provides a southern-situated perspective that argues that urban regeneration outcomes may not always be as benign as often depicted, particularly when dealing with existing settlements and informal activities, but nonetheless can also lead to positive outcomes. There are instances where regeneration efforts, in their failure to acknowledge and value the complexities of urban informality, adversely contribute to the erasure of spatial memory, the unjust displacement and dispossession of long-standing residents, and the erosion of communal neighbourhood spaces. But, on the other hand, they may also provide opportunities to some groups within such neighbourhoods to partake happily in the gentrification process, enabling them to become part of mainstream society. This combination of detrimental effects and long-awaited opportunities is evident in the case of Slave Island, an urban, high-density, and mixed-use district located at the core of the city of Colombo, where the outcomes of the ongoing Urban Regeneration Project have been traumatic and unsettling for many, while at the same time beneficial to some of those who live there. However, the project focussed only on impoverishment, turning a blind eye to the rich social networks, diversity of ethnicity and economies, and architectural and urban innovations that make Slave Island a ‘community’. To counteract their invisibility in the official urban regeneration projects, using in-depth interviews and on-site observations, this article registers the benefits, adverse costs, and unintended consequences of urban regeneration on such communities. It makes the case for revisiting the conventional ‘environmental cleansing’ approach of urban regeneration and instead, shifting towards a more sensitive ‘conservative surgery’ approach that celebrates the positive aspects of informality in those settlements. This shift helps create economic opportunities while preserving and respecting the rich traditions and sense of community and identity embedded in a unique urban setting such as Colombo’s Slave Island, and elsewhere.
DO  - 10.4324/9781003191407-13
UR  - https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003191407-13
