TI  - The importance of organisational memory—and how to sustain it
AU  - Piotr A. Bieńkowski
AU  - Hilary McGowan
PY  - 2024
AB  - ‘Organisational memory’ is an important, and often underestimated, aspect of being an effective learning organisation. It is the memory, experience and learning of the whole organisation, and not of one individual member of staff or volunteer, one team, a group of senior managers or a governing body. It is memory, learning and experiences that are acquired, retained and passed on to current and future generations of the organisation. In effect, it is how an organisation continues to learn over a long period of time, integrating past learning into present and future work. It has an important informational role, contributing to efficient and effective decision-making and ensuring that the same mistakes are not repeated.
