AU  - Roberto Rocha Coelho Pires
AU  - Maria Paula Bernardo dos Santos
AU  - Beatriz Almeida Brandão
AU  - Luiza Rosa
PY  - 2024
JO  - Policy Press eBooks
AB  - This chapter focuses on how weak institutional settings shape the possibilities for frontline work and for interactions with service users in a way conducive to the mistreatment of disadvantaged publics. It investigates a specific but rather common consequence of institutional weakness: implementation gaps or failures in putting into practice elements of formal policy designs. By examining the implementation of the National Policy on Care for Users of Alcohol and other Drugs in two Brazilian cities (Rio de Janeiro and Brasília), the chapter demonstrates that the insufficiency of essential resources and tools affects the behaviours and actions of frontline workers, leading to experiences of inattention, exclusion, or even harm by vulnerable service users. It outlines a research agenda focused on uncovering how policy implementation in weak institutional settings may contribute to perpetuating (rather than mitigating) inequalities, by reinforcing unequal access to public services.
