TI  - From ‘pop-up' to permanent
AU  - Quentin Stevens
AU  - Dale Leorke
AU  - Kim Dovey
AU  - Fauster Awepuga
AU  - Merrick Morley
PY  - 2025
AB  - Parklets and other tactical, pop-up forms of open space aim to better utilise existing streetscapes by reappropriating segments of space formerly dedicated to vehicular use and parking for green, pedestrian-only public spaces. By nature, these pop-up spaces are designed to be short-term, cheap and easily removable interventions that address immediate community needs and policy goals. But this chapter charts the developmental trajectories of four inner-city, temporary pop-up park projects that have become permanent. The chapter draws on analysis of local planning studies and policies, interviews with key actors, and government and media reports on how these pop-up parks performed. It also analyses the changing context of built form and activity in and around their sites, including the temporary and permanent space transformations themselves and the surrounding building uses and densities, building frontage conditions, existing public open spaces, street and transit networks, and traffic flows through them. The chapter shows how the locations, designs, and target users and uses of these four temporary open space interventions were prefigured by local governments’ wider analyses of local planning trends, problems and needs; how the projects were facilitated, validated and challenged by government planners, placemaking consultants and community actors; how the temporary interventions were translated into more permanent forms; and how the new open spaces have in turn helped to facilitate wider strategic urban development outcomes.
