TY  - CHAP
TI  - Vacancy and Ecosystem Services and Resilience
AU  - Justin B. Hollander
AU  - Olivia Carye Hallstein
AU  - Rui Zhu
AU  - Galen Newman
PY  - 2025
AB  - Vacant urban lands are often undervalued, yet they hold immense potential to enhance ecosystem services and urban resilience. These spaces can support biodiversity, regulate urban temperatures, facilitate rainwater infiltration, and mitigate flood risks. In their natural state, they provide essential habitats for flora and fauna, contributing to ecological balance. When intentionally regenerated through ecological restoration, sustainable landscaping, and green infrastructure, these lands amplify environmental benefits and strengthen urban sustainability. This chapter integrates foundational insights and presents future directions for vacancy regeneration related to ecosystem services and resilience, also drawing from nine significant journal articles to explore the role of vacant lands as critical ecological assets. Employing diverse methodologies – including field experiments, spatial analyses, and social–ecological assessments – these studies highlight the multifaceted contributions of vacant lands in supporting biodiversity, enhancing ecosystem functions, and shaping urban landscapes. Key findings reveal that urban vacant lands play a crucial role in biocontrol, carbon sequestration, and air pollution reduction while also providing opportunities for community-driven land-reuse initiatives. Despite these benefits, some challenges remain, including a lack of longitudinal research, geographic biases in case studies, and insufficient integration of economic and policy dimensions in ecological research. Addressing these gaps requires future studies to explore innovative approaches such as nature-based solutions, regenerative infrastructure, and renewable energy integration. Additionally, incorporating participatory design and community engagement can assist in fostering more inclusive and context-sensitive regeneration efforts.
