TI  - ALGORITHMIC ADMINISTRATION AND THE RIGHT TO A REASONED DECISION: DUE PROCESS, EXPLAINABILITY AND HUMAN REVIEW IN AUTOMATED PUBLIC GOVERNANCE
AU  - Şenyılmaz Doç. Dr. Dilek
PY  - 2026
JO  - Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research)
AB  - Public authorities increasingly use algorithmic systems to rank applications, detect fraud, allocate inspections, assess risk, calculate benefits, identify taxpayers, screen visa applicants and support decisions affecting liberty, property and access to public services. Automation may improve consistency and administrative capacity, but it also changes the structure of public power. A decision that appears to be made by an official may in fact depend on a model designed by a contractor, trained on historical data and protected by technical or commercial secrecy. Conventional administrative safeguards can become ineffective if the affected person cannot identify the decisive factors, contest inaccurate data or obtain meaningful human reconsideration.
