AB  - This chapter explores the role of competence, succession and organisational memory in MRT renewal programmes. It examines how experience and informal knowledge contribute to system stability, particularly in complex and constrained environments. The chapter considers how long-duration programmes are affected by staff turnover, role transitions and the loss of accumulated knowledge. It highlights the limitations of formal handover processes in capturing experiential understanding of system behaviour. The risks associated with fragmented delivery models and short-term resourcing are also examined. The chapter argues that competence should be understood as a system property rather than an individual attribute, and that maintaining continuity of knowledge and capability is essential for managing long-term renewal risk.
