PY  - 2026
JO  - Journal of Economics Finance and Management Studies
AB  - Developed and developing countries design multiple good, well intentioned policies, however these policies often failduring the implementation stage. This gap between the design part and the real outcome of the policy is called an implementation gap. Reports from the World Bank and UNDP show that weak governance, limited administrative capacity, and corruption reduce the effectiveness of welfare schemes, tax systems, and health programs. This study also shows that individuals within the system do not always act in a rational and logical manner. Their decisions and thoughts are influenced by daily habits, social norms, fear of loss, and limited information. For these reasons National governments of various countries are Using many behavioural tools such as nudges, default options, sending reminder messages, and campaigns to improve social policy outcomes on social norms. This paper argues that behavioural tools alone are not enough. The success of these tools depend largely on the capacity of institutions which are implementing them. Stronger institutions provide digital systems, trained personnel, monitoring systems and public trust to increase the impact of behaviour interventions. Whereas in weaker institutions, these tools may have only short-term or limited effects. This research paper mainly uses secondary data from reliable sources such as World Bank and mini government reports to do comparative case studies. Cases from India, Latin America, and Sub-Saharan Africa are examined to understand how behavioural tools perform under different levels of state capacity. It is found that when the behavioural tools are supported by strong governance and administrative measures are properly used the impact and outcome is better. Therefore, behavioural economics should be used as a complement to institutional reform, and not to replace it. Policymakers in developing countries should invest in both behavioural insights and institutional strengthening to reduce implementation gaps and to increase the effectiveness.
