TY  - JOUR
TI  - Knowledge retention and tacit knowledge in the public sector: a bibliometric and structural study
AU  - Guilherme Dutra Cassiano
AU  - Robson de Oliveira Albuquerque
AU  - Eduardo Amadeu Dutra Moresi
AU  - Hércules Antônio do Prado
PY  - 2026
JO  - Peer Review
AB  - Knowledge retention, especially tacit knowledge, is a challenge for public sector organizations facing functional mobility, staff turnover, retirements, leaves of absence, and employee unavailability. This article presents a bibliometric and structural study based on Scopus-indexed publications to map the evolution and conceptual structure of the literature on knowledge retention and tacit knowledge in the public sector. The corpus was examined through keyword co-occurrence and thematic clustering, considering mechanisms such as knowledge transfer and sharing, mentoring, succession planning, communities of practice, lessons learned, after-action reviews, and repositories. The results indicate the centrality of tacit knowledge and show that retention should be understood as a socio-technical phenomenon that integrates governance, learning practices, and informational infrastructure. The study identifies gaps related to terminological dispersion and low comparability of retention metrics, without empirically evaluating the effectiveness of the mechanisms.
