TY  - CHAP
TI  - The Problem of Implementation
AU  - J. A. Chandler
PY  - 2026
AB  - The previous chapters have described how central government constructs its policies towards local authorities. Not all the schemes of government, whether established as legislation, advice or through the allocation of financial resources, achieve their aims. Implementing policies concerning local authorities can be a particularly intractable problem for central governments in so far as the centre uses local authorities as an agent to undertake many functions it has sanctioned but does not choose to manage. Government policy can fail through mismanagement either at the centre or locality. Councillors can, moreover, deliberately choose not to carry out policies demanded by the centre.
