TY  - CHAP
TI  - The data-level bureaucracy
AU  - Rik Peeters
AU  - M.B. Schuilenburg
PY  - 2026
AB  - Two recent developments in the collection, distribution, and use of data have transformed the nature of digital government. First, digitalization has gone beyond government systems. Data, rather than being contained in organizational databases and datasets, has become part of data mobilities between both public and private parties. These data mobilities converge at sites (or nodes), and the links between these data mobilities create increasing complexity and frictions. Second, there is an increased use of artificial intelligence (AI) tools by governmental organizations at different administrative levels to analyse and process large volumes of data. Considering these developments, we propose the concept of the data-level bureaucracy to redefine the redistribution of authority and accountability for the execution of core tasks. In the data-level bureaucracy, data itself emerges as a semi-autonomous force, and a new sphere of discretionary power is created at the level of data analysts and AI developers.
