TY  - CHAP
TI  - The limits of the enabling strategy
AU  - Yap Kioe Sheng
PY  - 2026
AB  - This chapter examines the socio-political obstacles that hinder the full deployment of the enabling housing strategy, arguing that urban land markets are deeply embedded in prevailing socio-economic and political relationships rather than simple economic logic. It notes that the land market is often controlled by politically powerful, land-owning elites who benefit from high prices, while bureaucrats have vested interests in complex approval procedures. Based on a literature review of case studies of actual private-sector low-income housing projects, the chapter concludes that such housing rarely meets the needs and preferences of the low-income occupants because of its location in the urban fringe, necessitated by high land values. Given the middle-income housing shortage, whatever low-income housing is available tends to be downward-raided by middle-income households and gentrified. To maintain a low-income housing stock, such housing must be shielded from downward raiding by keeping it unattractive for middle-income groups, while maintaining crucial living standards.
