TY  - JOUR
TI  - Unlocking the middle zone: An efficiency framework for eco-compact development in post-industrial UK cities
AU  - Tianrui Sun
AU  - Cristian Silva
AU  - Jennifer M McKinley
PY  - 2026
JO  - Sustainable Cities and Society
AB  - Compact urban development has been widely promoted as a model for sustainable urbanism, yet evidence increasingly documents ecological concerns associated with elevated surface temperatures, worsening air quality, and shrinking green space. Current research has examined this tension predominantly through correlational approaches, assessing the direction and strength of associations between compact urban development and particular ecological outcomes. A different and more spatially diagnostic question, however, has received little attention: among all locations within a city, which lie on the best-achievable frontier defined by compactness and the three selected environmental conditions, and which consistently fall short? This study applies an efficiency lens to this question, using a Slacks-Based Measure (SBM) model at 500m grid scale across three UK post-industrial cities (Belfast, Glasgow, and Liverpool). The three environmental indicators are min–max normalised so that higher input values represent higher land surface temperature and PM2.5 and lower greenspace accessibility, while an expert-weighted Composite Compactness Index (CCI) integrating density, transport, and local accessibility is treated as the desired output. Results reveal a recurring zonal pattern across the three cities. Core zones record the highest mean and median eco-compact efficiency. Although middle and fringe zones show broadly comparable average efficiency, middle zones contain fewer frontier cells, revealing a middle-zone gap. By shifting attention from compactness-ecology relationships to their joint intra-urban performance, the study identifies middle-zone differentiation as a recurring pattern across selected post-industrial cities. The resulting maps also provide a spatial screening tool for regeneration planning.
DO  - 10.1016/j.scs.2026.107923
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1016/j.scs.2026.107923
